📝 Interview-Ready Explanation (One Paragraph)

This problem demonstrates how closures can be used to control function execution. By storing a flag in the outer scope, the returned function remembers whether it has already been called and prevents further executions. It also showcases higher-order functions and the decorator pattern, which are commonly used to extend functionality without modifying the original function.

🚀 Real-World Usage of This Concept

Ensure initialization logic runs once

Prevent duplicate API calls

One-time event handlers

Feature flags

Singleton patterns

🎯 Final Takeaway

If you fully understand this problem, you understand closures, which is one of the most important and frequently tested JavaScript concepts.
